**New evidence**
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Bangladesh","publisher":"U.S. Department of State","publication_date":"2023-03-20","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/de/dokument/2089131.html","claim":"The executive summary says the most significant issues included extrajudicial killings, torture, arbitrary detention, and forced disappearances by security forces, while civilian authorities maintained effective control over the security forces.","locator":"Executive Summary","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary U.S. government country report, mirrored in full text; the summary is explicit and directly relevant to 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Covers calendar year 2022 and was released in 2023.","ruler_attribution":"Directly relevant to Sheikh Hasina’s government because it describes the state security apparatus under her administration.","contrary_evidence":["The report also says civilian authorities maintained effective control over the security forces, which is a counterweight to the abuse findings."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.5","3B.10"]}

- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: Bangladesh","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/bangladesh","claim":"HRW says that after US sanctions, extrajudicial killings and enforced disappearances dropped dramatically, showing authorities could bring security-force abuses under control, but instead the government intensified threats and intimidation against human-rights defenders and families of disappeared persons.","locator":"Keynote / opening analysis","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Specialist human-rights monitor with direct Bangladesh chapter and a clear period summary.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Describes events of 2022 and the immediate aftermath.","ruler_attribution":"Applies to Hasina’s government and its control over security forces and intimidation campaigns.","contrary_evidence":["The same source is also critical of the government, so the positive capacity signal should not be overread as broad protection."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.10"]}

- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Amnesty International Report 2022/23: The state of the world’s human rights Bangladesh","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2023-03-27","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/documents/pol10/5670/2023/bn/","claim":"Amnesty reports that throughout 2022 serious attacks on freedom of expression and assembly continued, police used disproportionate and unnecessary force to suppress protests, violence against women and girls remained severe, and Rohingya protection remained a major challenge.","locator":"Bangladesh section and subheadings on expression, assembly, torture, women and girls, refugees","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Annual rights report with explicit 2022 coverage and Bangladesh-specific findings.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct calendar-year coverage for 2022.","ruler_attribution":"State conduct under Sheikh Hasina’s administration; the report is country-level but clearly government-related.","contrary_evidence":["The report also notes that some legal and policy changes were debated or partially advanced, so the picture is not one of uninterrupted escalation only."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.3","3B.5","3B.7","3B.8","3B.10"]}

- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh: Authorities must end repression of demonstrations","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-12-08","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/12/bangladesh-authorities-must-end-repression-of-demonstrations/","claim":"Amnesty said at least one person was killed, more than 60 were injured, and about 1,400 political activists had been arrested amid the authorities’ escalating crackdown on demonstrations.","locator":"Background and quoted statement by the South Asia regional director","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous rights organization statement tied to a dated protest crackdown.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Bangladesh authorities under Hasina’s government; the article discusses the state response, not isolated local misconduct.","contrary_evidence":["The piece is advocacy-oriented and relies partly on media reports for the incident count."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.5","3B.6","3B.8","3B.9","3B.10"]}

- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh: Crackdown on Political Opposition","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-10-10","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/10/10/bangladesh-crackdown-political-opposition","claim":"HRW reported that since August 22, 2022 at least four people had died and hundreds had been injured in clashes involving police, BNP supporters, and ruling-party supporters, while police carried out mass arrests of opposition supporters and ruling-party affiliates appeared to enjoy impunity for attacks.","locator":"Opening paragraphs and example of the Banani candlelight vigil assault","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct, dated reporting from a leading human-rights monitor with specific incident details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to Hasina’s government because it concerns police action and ruling-party-aligned impunity during opposition protests.","contrary_evidence":["The article also notes that each side accused the other of instigating violence, so not every clash is exclusively state-caused."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.5","3B.8","3B.9","3B.10"]}

- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"BNP-police clashes leave one dead as Naya Paltan turns battleground","publisher":"Prothom Alo","publication_date":"2022-12-07","url":"https://en.prothomalo.com/bangladesh/politics/mmdh40an9z","claim":"The report says the 7 December 2022 BNP-police clash left one man dead, more than 50 BNP supporters injured, and several hundred party leaders and activists arrested after police raided the party office.","locator":"Lead paragraphs and subsequent description of the raid","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous local reporting with specific casualty and arrest counts.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"State response under Hasina’s government; while local and immediate, the police action is clearly ruler-period relevant.","contrary_evidence":["The report is one event snapshot and does not by itself prove a nationwide pattern, though it fits the broader crackdown record."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.5","3B.8","3B.9","3B.10"]}

- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Digital security ACT: Cases filed mostly out of political motivation","publisher":"Prothom Alo","publication_date":"2022-09-19","url":"https://www.prothomalo.com/amp/story/bangladesh/0luicujl29","claim":"A CGS-linked analysis cited in the report said 47 percent of Digital Security Act cases were filed by police and ruling-party actors, and that the law was being used most heavily against journalists and opposition figures.","locator":"Opening paragraphs and quoted speakers in the report","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Local reporting that cites specific case data from a named research organization.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to Hasina’s government because the DSA was a state instrument used by police and ruling-party actors against critics.","contrary_evidence":["This is a case-pattern report rather than a court finding, so it shows abuse risk and use patterns more than final legal responsibility."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.5","3B.8","3B.10"]}

- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh: Officials Threaten Rohingya for Setting Up Schools","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-03-21","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/03/21/bangladesh-officials-threaten-rohingya-setting-schools","claim":"HRW said Bangladesh officials threatened to confiscate Rohingya refugees’ identity documents and forcibly relocate them to Bhasan Char if they violated the ban on refugee-led schools, affecting roughly 32,000 students after the December 2021 closures.","locator":"Opening paragraphs and the section on the school ban and threats","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct testimony-based NGO report with a very specific policy and consequence.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Events and policy consequences were active in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Bangladeshi authorities and the Hasina-era refugee-management policy.","contrary_evidence":["The report is strongest on coercion and restrictions; it does not show a successful remedy or an effective voluntary-resettlement alternative."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.5","3B.7","3B.8","3B.9","3B.10"]}

- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh Police Beating Rohingya Refugees","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-05-11","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/05/11/bangladesh-police-beating-rohingya-refugees","claim":"HRW reported that Armed Police Battalion officers beat Rohingya refugees at checkpoints, including people trying to obtain medicine, and described the harassment as part of efforts to coerce refugees toward Bhasan Char or return to Myanmar.","locator":"Opening paragraphs and the quoted refugee accounts","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct contemporaneous reporting with detailed witness testimony and a clear abuse pattern.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to Hasina’s government because the abuse was carried out by Bangladeshi security forces operating refugee policy.","contrary_evidence":["The report is specific to camp and checkpoint abuse, not the entire national territory."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.5","3B.7","3B.9","3B.10"]}

- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Allegations of enforced disappearance increase in Bangladesh","publisher":"Prothom Alo","publication_date":"2022-09-18","url":"https://en.prothomalo.com/bangladesh/pnj11b7krd","claim":"The UN Working Group report cited in the article said allegations of enforced disappearance in Bangladesh rose to 88 in the relevant period, while the government provided information on only eight victims and 81 cases remained unresolved.","locator":"Opening bullet points and the paragraph summarizing the UN report","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Local reporting on a UN mechanism with concrete numeric findings.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to Hasina’s government because it concerns state accountability for disappearances under her administration.","contrary_evidence":["These are allegations and reporting statistics, not final judicial findings on every case."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.10"]}

- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh: Stop Flouting UN Rights Concerns","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-03-17","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/03/17/bangladesh-stop-flouting-un-rights-concerns","claim":"HRW said UN experts had called on Bangladesh to end reprisals against human-rights defenders and relatives of forcibly disappeared persons, and warned that appointing officers accused of abuses to peacekeeping roles risked the credibility of those missions.","locator":"Body of the news release and the quoted UN-experts reference","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous rights-monitor summary of UN concern and state response.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to Hasina’s government because it concerns state response to defenders and families of victims.","contrary_evidence":["This is a secondary summary of UN concerns, not the UN text itself, though it is closely tied to the underlying record."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.4","3B.5","3B.10"]}

- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh: Stop Reprisals Against Victims, Activists","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-04-07","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/04/07/bangladesh-stop-reprisals-against-victims-activists","claim":"HRW reported that, after US sanctions on RAB, the Bangladesh government retaliated against victims’ relatives and human-rights defenders, and that the government used the Digital Security Act and the courts to harass critics.","locator":"Opening paragraphs and the section on the government response to sanctions","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct rights-monitor reporting with specific examples and named targets.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Hasina government’s response to external pressure and domestic criticism.","contrary_evidence":["The same episode also shows international pressure could force some restraint, but the retaliation itself remains the key point."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.4","3B.5","3B.8","3B.10"]}

- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh Government Keeps Rewarding Rights Abusers","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-10-05","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/10/05/bangladesh-government-keeps-rewarding-rights-abusers","claim":"HRW said Prime Minister Sheikh Hasina awarded medals and promotions to sanctioned RAB and police officials instead of ensuring accountability, sending the message that abuses would be rewarded rather than punished.","locator":"Middle paragraphs discussing promotions and awards","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct contemporaneous human-rights criticism with specific personnel actions and dates.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly tied to Hasina because the award decisions are described as hers and her government’s.","contrary_evidence":["The government could argue it was rewarding service, but the report squarely frames the action as rewarding abusers."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.4","3B.6","3B.10"]}

- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Human rights in Bangladesh","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/location/report-bangladesh/","claim":"Amnesty’s Bangladesh overview says freedom of expression, association, and assembly were unnecessarily restricted in 2022, with arbitrary arrests and harassment of critics, while women’s rights, Rohingya conditions, and protection for Indigenous Bawm people remained serious concerns.","locator":"2022 Bangladesh overview sections and background paragraphs","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Country-page synthesis of 2022 conditions from a major rights organization.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Summarizes 2022 and is updated as a country overview.","ruler_attribution":"Broadly attributable to the Hasina-era governing environment and its security practices.","contrary_evidence":["The overview is broad and summary-level, so it should not substitute for incident-level evidence."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.7","3B.8","3B.10"]}

- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Human Rights and Democracy: the 2022 Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office report","publisher":"UK Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/human-rights-and-democracy-report-2022/human-rights-and-democracy-the-2022-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-report","claim":"The UK report says Bangladesh’s human-rights situation remained a serious concern in 2022, but also notes fewer reports of attacks on people because of religion or belief and a reduction in reports of extrajudicial killings and abductions.","locator":"Bangladesh country subsection","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official annual foreign-policy human-rights report with explicit Bangladesh section and year coverage.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly about 2022.","ruler_attribution":"State-level assessment of the Hasina government’s domestic safety environment.","contrary_evidence":["The same report emphasizes ongoing harassment, detention, media restrictions, and violence against women and girls, so the positive lines are only partial offsets."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.6","3B.7","3B.10"]}

- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh Police grievance redress system / citizen charter","publisher":"Bangladesh Police","publication_date":"2022-11-21 or current portal text","url":"https://www.police.gov.bd/index.php/en/citizen_charter","claim":"The police citizen charter sets out formal complaint and grievance-redress routes, including GRS escalation, district and metropolitan complaint channels, and women/children/elderly service desks.","locator":"Section titled 'অভিযোগ ব্যবস্থাপনা পদ্ধতি (GRS)' and the listed complaint services","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official police-service portal describing formal complaint handling.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"The page reflects an active grievance system and is consistent with the 2022-2023 service environment.","ruler_attribution":"Institutional context under the Hasina government, not proof of effective remedy.","contrary_evidence":["A formal complaint channel does not prove independence, accessibility, or effective follow-through in abuse cases."],"lenses":["3B.4"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
- I considered roughly 30 documents across HRW, Amnesty, the U.S. State Department, the UK FCDO annual report, UN mechanism references, Prothom Alo, The Daily Star, and Bangladesh Police portals.
- I rejected repeated or near-duplicate protest stories that restated the same 7-December or August-December crackdown without adding new facts.
- I rejected later 2023-2026 retrospectives on protests, disappearances, and the new regime because they do not improve 2022 attribution for this chapter.
- I rejected broad economic, climate, and international-security pieces unless they tied directly to domestic physical safety, repression, or vulnerable-group protection.
- I rejected several weak or blocked direct opens where the source page was unavailable or technical, unless a stable mirrored text or snippet already contained the needed claim.
- I rejected multiple generic Bangladesh Police pages that listed services without a clear complaint or remedy mechanism relevant to abuse accountability.
- I kept the strongest mix as: one official country report, three major rights-monitor summaries, two protest-crackdown reports, two Rohingya-protection reports, one disappearances update, one rights-abuser reward report, one UK annual report, and one official grievance mechanism page.

**Remaining gaps**
- The main gap is direct chain-of-command evidence for specific 2022 abuse orders from Sheikh Hasina herself. The record strongly shows state responsibility, but not always a personal order trail.
- Remedy and oversight remain weakly evidenced. We have formal grievance pathways, but little hard proof that they were independent or effective in 2022.
- The record on minority protection is mixed and more general than I would like; more targeted 2022 evidence on communal violence, Indigenous protection, or women’s safety could sharpen the attribution.
- More searching would help if the goal is to distinguish routine local policing from centrally directed repression, especially around the December opposition crackdown and Rohingya camp enforcement.
